    <DataNode elementId="CPD-22659" textLabel="trimethylarsane" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="HTDIUWINAKAPER-UHFFFAOYSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: CPD-22659 Trimethylarsine| (trimethylarsine) exists under standard conditions as a gas. It was first synthesized in 1854. It has been suggested that this gas was responsible for an epidemic of sudden infant deaths that took place in Europe in the early 1830s, resulting from fumes produced by the molds growing on wallpapers decorated with arsenical colors such as |FRAME: CPD-24330| and |FRAME: CPD-24333|. While there has been accumulating suspicion that the source of the poisoning was the arsenical compounds |CITS: [17745436]|, an exact mechanism of delivery was proposed by the Italian scientist Bartolomeo Gosio (1863-1944), who suggested that mold growing on the wallpaper converts the solid arsenical compounds into a volatile form. One of the mold strains identified by Gosio as responsilbe for this transformation is |FRAME: TAX-561152| |CITS: [17745435][Gosio93]|. Gosio incorrectly identified the main volatile product as |FRAME: CPD-24328| |CITS: [Gosio01]|. The true identity of the fungal volatile product as |FRAME: CPD-22659| was reported only in 1933 by the English chemist Frederick Challenger |CITS: [Challenger33]|, but |FRAME: CPD-22659| is still often referred to as Gosio gas |CITS: [17733573]|. Ironically, |FRAME: CPD-22659| is actually not very toxic. Its LC&lt;sub&gt;50&lt;/sub&gt; value is 20,000 ppm while that for |FRAME: CPD-24327| is only 0.05 ppm. It is now beleived that the gas was not the causative agent of the illness in the 19th century |CITS: [15693178]|. A potential explanation may be the presence of the mold |FRAME: TAX-74722|, which produces airborne |FRAME: Mycotoxins mycotoxins| such as the |FRAME: Tricothecenes tricothecenes| |CITS: [11798344][15640178][16269780]|.</Comment>
